00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.150
Nicole: So how do the ISO
standards support cyber security?

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:11.129
A cyber security professional will
probably find the ISO/IEC 27001 standard

00:00:11.469 --> 00:00:15.239
details most of the requirements
that a business needs to follow to

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:18.549
comply with ISO 27000 standards.

00:00:19.109 --> 00:00:22.130
Abraham: It sets out the
specifications that an Information

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:27.280
Security Management System [ISMS]
must meet to become certified.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.349
An ISMS is the set of policies and
procedures including legal, physical

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.700
and technical controls involved in the
information risk management process.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:41.550
Nicole: Those organisations that
meet the standards can become

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:47.550
certified as ISO 27001 compliant by
an accredited certification body.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.460
Abraham: ISO 27001
requires organisations to:

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.019
• Systematically examine information
security risks looking at threats,

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.120
vulnerabilities and impact.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:03.850
• Design and implement security controls
to address unacceptable risks.

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.989
• Develop a comprehensive management process
to regularly review and update the ISMS

00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.940
taking into consideration the threat
environment and other developments.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.690
Nicole: The ISO 27001 also includes
guidelines for developing controls

00:01:19.710 --> 00:01:24.020
in a range of areas such as
information security policies, human

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.550
resource security, asset management,
operations and communications security

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:31.800
and compliance amongst others.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:38.489
Abraham: By aligning an organisations
ISMS to the ISO 27001 standard it

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:42.530
can also help to show that measures
have been taken to comply with GDPR.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.119
Nicole: In the event of a data breach
happening an ISMS that conforms to ISO

00:01:48.139 --> 00:01:53.880
27001 will demonstrate to the regulator
that they have done all they can to

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.060
prevent and recover from an attack.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:03.919
Abraham: The ISO 27032 standard is
usually used together with ISO 27001 and

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:08.480
includes guidelines on information and
data protection to make sure there are no

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:10.940
gaps in security policies and procedures

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:15.530
Nicole: It focuses on information systems
and guidelines to prevent data leaks,

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.879
like encrypting communication channels.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:22.170
The emphasis is more on network
and internet security and critical

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.539
information infrastructure.

00:02:24.289 --> 00:02:28.430
Abraham: The ISO 27000 family as we
have said covers a range of different

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:32.189
standards that can help organisations
to develop different aspects of their

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:35.019
security controls and ISMS systems.

00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:41.810
Nicole: ISO standards provide best
practice guidance so having ISO 27000

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:46.230
certification can help organisations
to show that they use effective

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:48.250
security practices and procedures.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.710
This can in turn give employees
and clients assurance that

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.429
their data is in safe hands.

00:02:55.200 --> 00:02:58.350
Abraham: Some organisations will
also have to show that they are ISO

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:02.540
certified as a condition of doing
business, this is especially true

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:04.110
when working with government bodies.

